@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "Migrate a record end to end") {
        Take an ATProto Lexicon document, turn it into a schema, state a
        migration on that schema, check it, compile it, and carry a real
        post record through it and back out as JSON.

        The migration here is the identity, which is the one migration
        whose answer you already know: what comes out should be the
        record that went in. That makes it the right shape to learn on,
        because every stage can be checked against something rather than
        taken on trust. Swapping the identity for a mapping that changes
        the schema changes one line of what you write below.

        You need two documents: an `app.bsky.feed.post` Lexicon and one
        post record. The panproto repository carries both, under
        `fixtures/atproto/lexicons/` and `fixtures/atproto/records/`, and
        `Examples/AtprotoPostMigration` is this program reading them.
    }

    @Section(title: "Read the inputs and parse the lexicon") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Start with a program that does no panproto work at all, then
            add the first call that allocates an engine resource. A
            Lexicon document is ATProto's own surface syntax, and parsing
            it is what turns a protocol's format into the pointed graph
            every later stage speaks.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create a file with the two module imports and an entry
                point that takes both document paths.

                `PanprotoStructural` carries the values every engine call
                takes and returns, so both imports are wanted from the
                start. Run it against the two documents and it reports
                `read 3295 bytes of lexicon` and `read 281 bytes of
                record`.

                @Code(name: "AtprotoPostMigration.swift", file: migrate-01.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Wrap the body in ``PanprotoEngine/run(_:)`` and parse the
                lexicon.

                Everything inside the closure runs on the engine thread
                with no suspension between calls, which is what lets
                `defer { schemaHandle.release() }` return the slab entry
                at the end of the scope; see <doc:TheEngineActor> and
                <doc:HandleLifecycle>. The lexicon reads as 39 vertices
                and 39 edges in the `atproto` protocol, entered at
                `app.bsky.feed.post`, and that entry is the pointing: it
                names the vertex an instance of this schema is rooted at.

                @Code(name: "AtprotoPostMigration.swift", file: migrate-02.swift)
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "State the migration and check it") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A migration is stated, checked, and only then compiled. The
            three are separate because they answer different questions:
            what maps to what, whether that mapping is admissible, and
            how a record is carried through it.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Build the identity migration on the schema.

                `Migration.identity(on:)` maps every vertex, edge, and
                hyper-edge to itself. It is an identity at one schema and
                nowhere else, which is why it is derived from the schema
                rather than written down as a constant. This runs in the
                value layer; no engine call is made.

                @Code(name: "AtprotoPostMigration.swift", file: migrate-03.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Ask whether the mapping exists.

                ``PanprotoStructural/Migration/checkExistence(against:from:to:)``
                asks whether the mapping satisfies the obligations the
                ATProto protocol's theories impose. A failing check is
                still a successful call: the verdict arrives in
                `ExistenceReport.valid` and the reasons in
                `ExistenceReport.errors`, so the report is read rather
                than caught.

                @Code(name: "AtprotoPostMigration.swift", file: migrate-04.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Compile it.

                Compiling settles which vertices and edges survive and
                how each arc is re-resolved, so that carrying a record
                through is a lookup rather than a search. The handle it
                answers with is also what a lens is: `get`, `put`, and
                the law checkers all take one of these.

                @Code(name: "AtprotoPostMigration.swift", file: migrate-05.swift)
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Carry a record through") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            With a compiled migration in hand, the record can be read in
            through ATProto's codec, lifted, and written back out. This
            is the loop that closes: what comes out is a post record
            again.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Parse the record against the schema.

                The schema is an argument because the same bytes read
                against a different schema would be a different instance.
                ``SchemaHandle/violations(in:)`` then checks the parsed
                instance against the schema it claims to be an instance
                of, which for this record reports none.

                @Code(name: "AtprotoPostMigration.swift", file: migrate-06.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Lift it through the compiled migration.

                The identity preserves the nodes and the arcs, though the
                engine rebuilds the arc list in its own order, so the two
                agree as sets rather than as sequences. That is worth
                noticing early: an instance's arc list is a set with an
                incidental order, not a sequence to compare position by
                position.

                @Code(name: "AtprotoPostMigration.swift", file: migrate-07.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Emit the lifted instance and compare it against the
                input.

                Emitting through the codec that parsed the input closes
                the ingestion loop, and the output is smaller than the
                input because the codec writes the record without the
                input's whitespace, which is why the comparison is over
                parsed fields rather than over bytes. Run the whole
                program and it reports 5 nodes and 4 arcs preserved
                across the lift, with `text`, `createdAt`, and `langs`
                agreeing afterwards: `pipeline OK: in=281 bytes ->
                out=256 bytes`.

                @Code(name: "AtprotoPostMigration.swift", file: migrate-08.swift)
            }
        }
    }

    @Assessments {
        @MultipleChoice {
            The pipeline never asks the engine to compose two migrations,
            yet a host with a release history usually has several. Where
            does that composition belong?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                In the value layer, before anything is compiled.

                @Justification(reaction: "Correct") {
                    `Migration.composed(with:)` folds specifications
                    without an engine, so a host pays for one compile
                    rather than one per revision. Composition there drops
                    on a miss, which is why a field-level edit is an
                    amendment to a self-map.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Only after each step has been compiled separately.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite") {
                    Compiled migrations do compose, and
                    ``CompiledMigrationHandle/composedLens(with:)`` is the
                    call for it when the value-level work matters. But
                    compiling each step first costs a compile per
                    revision, which folding the specifications avoids.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Nowhere: a migration between two versions has to be
                written out in full.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite") {
                    ``PanprotoStructural/Migration`` is a semigroup under
                    composition, so a release history folds into one
                    mapping. What it is not is a monoid: the empty
                    mapping annihilates rather than units.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            The lift reports `arcs preserved: true` only because the
            program compares `Set(lifted.arcs)` against
            `Set(instance.arcs)`. What would comparing the arrays
            directly have told you?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Nothing reliable: the engine rebuilds the arc list in its
                own order.

                @Justification(reaction: "Correct") {
                    An instance's arcs are a set with an incidental order.
                    The same holds for the bytes an engine writes for a
                    schema, for the same underlying reason.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                That the lift reordered the record's fields.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite") {
                    Field order in the emitted JSON is the codec's, and
                    the arc list's order is the engine's; neither is
                    something the lift chose.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                That the identity migration is not actually an identity.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite") {
                    The identity maps every vertex and edge to itself, and
                    the nodes do compare equal as an array. Only the arc
                    ordering is incidental.
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
